@@ -0,0 +1,82 @@
+# Daily Grind security audit
+
+The site has one HTML document, local CSS, and one existing HTTPS Unsplash
+background image. It has no client-side JavaScript, DOM injection sinks, inline
+event handlers, authentication implementation, session store, forms, frames,
+popups, service workers, analytics, or third-party executable dependencies.
+
+| Finding | Implemented protection |
+| --- | --- |
+| Inline style block and style attribute required a permissive style policy | Moved all styles into `/styles.css`; no inline style exceptions |
+| No content security policy | Enforced deny-by-default CSP; scripts, connections, objects, frames, and workers blocked; local CSS and the existing image origin allowed |
+| Future DOM XSS sinks could be introduced | Trusted Types required and all policy creation disabled; no permissive default policy |
+| Missing response protections | Framing denied, MIME sniffing disabled, referrers suppressed, unused permissions disabled, CORP same-origin, COOP same-origin-allow-popups |
+| Logout only displayed text | Clears cookies, storage, and cache; expires the configured root session cookie; responses are not cached |
+| Arbitrary paths returned the homepage | Explicit asset and existing page route allowlists; unknown paths return 404; unsupported methods return 405 |
+| Cross-site resource and logout requests | Fetch Metadata rejects cross-site and same-site subresource requests and logout; ordinary incoming document links remain usable |
+| No policy regression visibility | CSP reports supported through Reporting API and legacy `report-uri`; bounded parsing and minimal structured summaries |
+
+## Running and deployment
+
+`npm start` runs the dependency-free Node HTTP server. `PORT` defaults to 11222.
+`npm run build` syntax-checks the server files without executing their entrypoints
+or opening a socket. Deploy behind a reverse proxy that terminates HTTPS and
+enforces HTTPS redirects. The app does not trust arbitrary forwarded headers.
+
+Set `HTTPS_ONLY=1` after HTTPS is configured. This enables a short initial
+`Strict-Transport-Security: max-age=300`; increase the duration after deployment
+has been validated. Subdomain coverage and HSTS preload are deliberately not
+enabled without knowing the deployment's domain configuration.
+
+`SECURITY_POLICY_MODE=enforce` is the default, suitable for the audited script-free
+page. For future dependency or policy changes, use
+`SECURITY_POLICY_MODE=report-only` in staging or a controlled rollout, review
+reports across representative traffic, then restore `enforce`. Report-only mode
+retains enforced framing, base URL, and object restrictions plus companion
+headers. It does not enforce the full CSP or Trusted Types restrictions.
+
+`/security-reports` accepts POST bodies of at most 16 KiB and at most 20 reports
+in either CSP report or Reporting API format. Only directive and disposition
+are logged. URLs, referrers, samples, credentials, and raw bodies are never
+logged. This script-free policy does not request script samples. Treat reports
+as untrusted diagnostics rather than proof of exploitation; filter extension
+noise and apply request/log rate limits at the reverse proxy before exposing
+the endpoint publicly. Reports and the Reporting API depend on browser support
+and secure contexts; `report-uri` provides a compatibility path.
+
+## Logout and authentication boundary
+
+The existing Logout link still requests `/logout`. GET and POST clear browser
+state and show a confirmation; HEAD and speculative prefetch/prerender requests
+do not mutate it. Cross-site and same-site requests are rejected when Fetch
+Metadata is available. Clients without Fetch Metadata retain access.
+
+The fallback cookie expiration defaults to `__Host-session`, with `Path=/`,
+`Secure`, `HttpOnly`, and `SameSite=Lax`. Set `SESSION_COOKIE_NAME` if the deployed
+site uses a different cookie name. Cookie deletion must match the original
+cookie's name, path, and domain; this fallback assumes a host-only root cookie.
+`Clear-Site-Data` handles other browser cookies and storage on supporting secure
+contexts, but browser support varies and local plain HTTP is insufficient for
+production logout guarantees. Clearing cookies may affect other applications
+on the same registrable domain, so use a dedicated deployment domain.
+
+There is currently no server-side authenticated session to revoke. If login or
+an external identity provider is added, revoke the session/token in that system
+before returning a successful logout response; browser cleanup alone cannot
+invalidate a copied token. Prefer a same-origin POST form with CSRF protection
+when authenticated sessions are introduced.
+
+Trusted Types restrictions are progressive defense: older browsers may ignore
+them. The site remains safe without a polyfill because it executes no client
+scripts and uses no HTML injection sinks. No sanitizer dependency or permissive
+Trusted Types policy is needed. Cross-origin embedder isolation is omitted
+because the site requires no shared memory features and the external image
+does not need that deployment risk.
+
+## Validation scope
+
+Syntax checks and source inspection cover route/method handling, explicit file
+exposure, markup/CSP compatibility, the third-party resource inventory, and
+report data handling. No server, server entrypoint import, or browser session
+was used during this change. Runtime behavior and browser policy enforcement
+have not been exercised.
